      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, absolutely! The angle of attack is the primary control for how much lift a wing generates, and it directly dictates the strength of the downwash.\n\nWhen you tilt the nose of the plane up, you increase the angle of attack. This forces the wing to push against the oncoming air more aggressively. As the angle increases, the wing deflects a larger volume of air downward with greater force. According to Newton's Third Law, that increased downward push on the air creates a proportionally stronger upward reaction force on the wing, resulting in more lift.\n\nHowever, there is a limit to this. If you tilt the nose up too much, the airflow can separate from the top of the wing, creating a stall where lift drops dramatically. But within the normal operating range, yes, a steeper angle of attack means stronger downwash and more lift.\n\nIt's a bit like trying to push a heavy cart uphill; the steeper the slope (the angle), the harder you have to push (the downwash) to keep moving forward, but if you go too steep, you might lose your footing (stall).",
